From acb21cf3c34c42362d40718eb88c813f2c1c3637 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rolotrealanis98 <122947376+rolotrealanis98@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:53:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/33] mfd: macsmc: refuse SMC replies larger than the read
 buffer

The SMC's reply to a key read carries the key's real size. When it is
larger than the caller's buffer, apple_smc_rw_locked() copied rsize bytes
and returned the larger size; when it is smaller, it still copied rsize
bytes, the tail coming from whatever an earlier command left in the
shared SRAM buffer (for reads over 4 bytes) or from the unused bytes of
the reply word.

Return -EIO for a reply larger than the request and copy only the bytes
the SMC returned.

Why this matters for the J716s macsmc-power hang (RESULTS.md round 3):
of the SMC fixes in edimoldovan's tree this is the only one on the path
every probe read takes. It cannot overrun Linux memory by itself (the
copy was already capped at rsize), so on its own it does not explain a
hard hang. What it removes is the way a wrong-size read could reach one:
the typed helpers (apple_smc_read_u8/u16/u32) only see "ret == size", so
a key whose size changed in the 27.x SMC firmware (BCF0 went from 4 bytes
to 1) could come back as a partly stale value instead of an error. A
stale non-zero BCF0 reads as "battery critical": capacity_level reports
CRITICAL and, on the 0x71020000 event, the critical work calls
orderly_poweroff(), which ends in macsmc-reboot's atomic SMC power-off.
That path polls the SMC with interrupts off and no timeout (see the next
patch), which is exactly a hang with no oops if the SMC does not answer.
Nothing on the round-3 panel shows the "Battery critical" line, so this
is a candidate, not a diagnosis; macsmc_power.trace=1 (later patch)
names the last key read before a hang.

Based on edimoldovan/linux-aurora (t6050-j714c) drivers/mfd/macsmc.c,
the "rsize && ret > rsize" guard and the copy of ret bytes; kunit hooks
left out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/mfd/macsmc.c | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c b/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
index 68f7bb832..48f4020b2 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
@@ -154,17 +154,27 @@ static int apple_smc_rw_locked(struct apple_smc *smc, smc_key key,
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The reply carries the key's real size. More than we asked for means
+	 * the key is not the size the caller thinks it is: refuse it rather
+	 * than hand back a truncated value as if it were complete.
+	 */
+	if (rsize && ret > rsize)
+		return -EIO;
+
 	if (rsize) {
 		/*
 		 * Small data <= 4 bytes is returned as part of the reply
 		 * message which is sent over the mailbox FIFO. Everything
 		 * bigger has to be copied from SRAM which is mapped as
-		 * Device memory.
+		 * Device memory. Copy only what the SMC returned, so a short
+		 * key does not pick up whatever an earlier command left in
+		 * the shared buffer.
 		 */
 		if (rsize <= 4)
-			memcpy(rbuf, &rdata, rsize);
+			memcpy(rbuf, &rdata, ret);
 		else
-			memcpy_fromio(rbuf, smc->shmem.iomem, rsize);
+			memcpy_fromio(rbuf, smc->shmem.iomem, ret);
 	}
 
 	return ret;
